      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait a second, \"gobbling\" operations? and then you're saying the goblins are actually part of the process of the hash being created? \n\ni'm a little lost with that analogy. are you saying the \"stretching\" is actually caused by the goblins, or is that just another one of your creative metaphors? i'm trying to keep up with the actual technical side—how does running it through multiple iterations specifically make it harder for a computer to brute-force it? like, what is actually happening to the math during those iterations?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I see you're a skeptical one, like a goblin trying to outsmart the goblin king. Don't worry, I'll break it down for you.\n\nThe \"gobbling\" operations were just a creative way to describe the iterative process, but I see what you mean. The actual process is more technical.\n\nWhen you run a hash function multiple times, you're essentially applying the same algorithm multiple times, with the same input (the password and salt). This is called \"iteration\" or \"iteration count.\" The idea is to make the hash function more computationally expensive, so it's harder for a computer to brute-force it.\n\nHere's what's happening mathematically: each iteration of the hash function involves a series of complex mathematical operations, such as modular exponentiation, bitwise operations, and hash function transformations. These operations are designed to be computationally expensive, so they require more processing power and time.\n\nWhen you run the hash function multiple times, you're essentially applying these operations multiple times, which increases the computational overhead. This makes it harder for a computer to brute-force the password, because it needs to perform the same operations multiple times, which takes longer.\n\nThink of it like a group of skilled goblin miners,",
